        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(qqbot): add actionable setup error guidance",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nAdd shared, account-aware QQBot setup guidance to credential, token, API, and network failures.\n\nFixes #65868.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nQQBot failures previously left users with terse or raw errors. The first version of this PR made those errors actionable, but always named `QQBOT_APP_ID` and `QQBOT_CLIENT_SECRET`. Those environment variables resolve only for the default account, so that advice could not repair a named account.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nQQBot config resolution intentionally distinguishes the default account from named accounts:\n\n- default: `channels.qqbot.appId` plus `clientSecret`/`clientSecretFile`, with `QQBOT_APP_ID` and `QQBOT_CLIENT_SECRET` fallback;\n- named: `channels.qqbot.accounts.<id>.appId` plus `clientSecret`/`clientSecretFile`, without environment fallback.\n\nThe setup helper now accepts account context for missing-credential paths. Context-free token/API failures use account-neutral guidance rather than suggesting default-only environment variables.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Default-account startup and outbound failures show both supported config and environment recovery paths.\n- Named-account failures show the exact account-scoped config path and do not mention ignored `QQBOT_*` variables.\n- Token, structured API, and network failures retain the docs link and actionable account-neutral credential guidance.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Make missing-credential guidance account-aware.\n- Pass `account.accountId` from gateway, text outbound, and media outbound paths.\n- Make token/API/network guidance account-neutral.\n- Add focused regression tests for default, named, and context-free guidance.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Real behavior proof\n\nExecuted the production helper through Node/tsx after the fix (synthetic account IDs; no credentials):\n\n```text\nnamed-account runtime output:\nQQBot not configured (missing appId or clientSecret). Set channels.qqbot.accounts.operations.appId and clientSecret (or clientSecretFile), then see https://docs.openclaw.ai/channels/qqbot\ndefault-account runtime output:\nQQBot not configured (missing appId or clientSecret). Set channels.qqbot.appId and clientSecret (or clientSecretFile), or set QQBOT_APP_ID and QQBOT_CLIENT_SECRET, then see https://docs.openclaw.ai/channels/qqbot\n```\n\nThis demonstrates that named-account runtime output no longer advertises the default-only environment variables.\n\n### Validation\n\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run extensions/qqbot/src/engine/config/setup-guidance.test.ts extensions/qqbot/src/engine/api/token.test.ts extensions/qqbot/src/engine/api/api-client.test.ts` — passed (3 files, 14 tests).\n- `./node_modules/.bin/oxfmt --check <six changed QQBot files>` — passed.\n- `./node_modules/.bin/oxlint --tsconfig config/tsconfig/oxlint.extensions.json <six changed QQBot files>` — passed (0 warnings, 0 errors).\n- `git diff HEAD^ --check` — passed.\n- `gitleaks git --no-banner --redact --log-opts='HEAD^..HEAD' --exit-code 1` — passed, no leaks found.\n- Patch-file `gitleaks dir --redact` scan before commit — passed, no leaks found.\n\n### Typecheck note\n\nA direct local `node scripts/run-tsgo.mjs -p extensions/qqbot/tsconfig.json --noEmit` was attempted but is not valid in this linked worktree: the reused dependency tree cannot resolve the checkout's `openclaw/plugin-sdk/*` workspace exports, producing broad pre-existing module-resolution failures. Focused tests and targeted lint cover the changed surface; hosted CI remains authoritative for the full extension type lane.\n",
        "branch": "lhk/qqbot-actionable-errors-65868",
